            <ArticleTitle>Two-year follow-up with acoustic rhinometry in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>203-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This is an explorative study of growth-related nasal geometry in healthy children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 88 girls and boys between the ages of 4 and 16 years were followed over a period of 2 years by acoustic rhinometry. The registrations were correlated to height at each occasion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Although both the minimal cross-sectional area and the nasal volume increased in all age groups over 7 years, large individual variations in nasal geometry led to a weak relationship with growth.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the undecongested nasal cavity in children, correlation of nasal geometry makes sense for height but not for age.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
